      "claim": "The most relevant L1/L2 contexts for the query's methylsulfonylphenyl/heteroaryl scaffold show human absolute bioavailability at or above 30%: esaxerenone 89% fasted, basmisanil ~75%, BI 425809 71.6%, odanacatib 30% at 50 mg, and bitopertin inferred high in humans; these affirmatively support F >= 20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C01","C20","C02","C05","C08"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L4 absorption/permeability evidence for related compounds (esaxerenone high absorption ratio; vismodegib high-permeability BCS class II) supports adequate absorption, and L5 records for methylsulfonylphenyl-containing reference molecules (rofecoxib, BI 730357) show high permeability with efflux ratios near 1, providing weak mechanistic support against gut-wall efflux.",
      "card_ids": ["C58","C62","C67","C75","C73","C79"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The only sub-20% values in the visible evidence are bitopertin rat microsuspension values of 14% and 23%, which are low-transferability animal formulation data and do not outweigh the human high-bioavailability evidence for related compounds.",
      "card_ids": ["C31","C35"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Solubility/dissolution remains a possible limitation (high logP, low Fsp3, vismodegib poor solubility), but no affirmative threshold-relevant evidence supports F < 20% for the query.",
      "card_ids": ["C62","C67"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "gut_wall_efflux_intestinal_metabolism",
      "applicability": "low",
      "direction": "supportive",
      "decision_effect": "no_change",
      "card_ids": ["C68","C69","C70","C71","C72","C73","C74","C75","C76","C77","C78","C79"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct oral bioavailability or matched oral/IV AUC measurement exists for the query molecule itself.",
    "No query-specific gut-wall efflux or intestinal metabolism measurements are available; L5 records are for unrelated reference molecules.",
    "Query-specific solubility and dissolution are not directly measured; high logP and low Fsp3 suggest possible dissolution limitation.",
    "No query-specific first-pass metabolism or systemic clearance data are available.",
    "Transfer from related methylsulfonylphenyl/heteroaryl compounds is qualitative and does not establish the query's exact F value."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Prediction remains high: L5 adds no query-specific gut-wall efflux or intestinal metabolism evidence; its Caco-2 records for other molecules mostly show high permeability and no efflux, which is weakly supportive but not decisive. The decision rests on related methylsulfonylphenyl/heteroaryl compounds with human absolute bioavailability values of 30-89% and high-permeability classifications; the only sub-20% values are low-transferability rat microsuspension data. Confidence remains moderate."
}

